# DECLINE GRADER (R0123). The line above promises the model that "a PASS is also scored ... marked
# against the same horizon as a real call"; record_call kept that promise only for CALLs, and the
# sleeve responded by writing NINE consecutive PASSes and logging ZERO scoreable forecasts. A model
# graded only on the trades it chooses to be graded on has a dominant strategy: never trade. This
# grades the declines -- the counterfactual direction the model stated it WOULD have taken --
# against the realised move over the same window, and reports whether the filter ADDS value or
# merely avoids deciding. The good answer is a coin flip: a 50/50 trade loses money once fees are
# paid, so declining it was correct. Declines are logged under their own kind and are excluded from
# the calibration pool that shrinks live Kelly sizing -- they must never move position size on
# trades the desk never took.
# CADENCE CEILING: DATA-ARRIVAL. A row becomes gradeable only when its own 8h horizon elapses, and
# rows arrive every 4h from the line above, so maturation events happen every 4h and no more often.
# Phased to :25 -- ten minutes past the 08:15 maturity of a 00:15 decision, and clear of
# score_forecasts at :50. Running hourly would re-read unchanged state three times in four.
# EVIDENCE: scripts/resolve_llm_trader_book.py -> reports/llm_trader_decline_value.json; row R0123
# CONFIDENCE: committed-script (new this cycle)
# RETIRED 2026-09-05 under the MT5 universe mandate (2026-08-18):
# scripts/resolve_llm_trader_book.py was a research/execution organ of the retired
